--TEST--
`Core\Cldr::pluralCategory` refuses a language whose rules it does not carry, rather than answering another language's — ADR 0082 § 2, ADR 0095
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0095: ambiguous input is refused, never repaired. The cheaper design
// here answers English's `one`/`other` for anything it does not recognize,
// and it is wrong in the direction that hides: a Russian catalog written
// against those two forms reads correctly for 1 and wrongly for 2, 5 and 11
// alike. The refusal names the subtag, so the gap arrives as a message.
try {
    Core\Cldr::pluralCategory(2, "tlh");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// A tag carrying no language subtag is the same refusal and not a silent
// `other`.
try {
    Core\Cldr::pluralCategory(2, "-GB");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// The refusal is about the roster and not about the member: a language the
// table does carry still answers, and answers its own rules.
echo "pl 2: ", Core\Cldr::pluralCategory(2, "pl") == Core\Cldr\PluralCategory::Few ? "few" : "other", "\n";
echo "pl 5: ", Core\Cldr::pluralCategory(5, "pl") == Core\Cldr\PluralCategory::Many ? "many" : "other", "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Cldr::pluralCategory carries no plural rules for the language `tlh`: a language is never given another one's rules, because a catalog written against the wrong forms reads correctly for one count and wrongly for the rest
Core\Cldr::pluralCategory read no language subtag from the locale `-GB`: a tag begins with letters
pl 2: few
pl 5: many
